ions.\nThe F3/G3 area is just too dangerous to mess with right now. I have to avoid a bad trade.\n\nSo, the play is (D,2) -> (D,3). This secures the board in the long-term.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: The current board state shows the opponent with a threatening cluster on the right side (G1, G2, E3, F4). The most critical threat is the opponent's potential move E3 -> D3, which would capture three of your pieces (C2, D2, E2). Attacks into the opponent's territory at F3 or G3 are perilous because they open up devastating counter-jumps for the opponent (e.g., G1 -> G3) that would flip multiple pieces. Therefore, the priority is defense and containment. By cloning to D3, you block the opponent's best move, capture E3 (temporarily), and force a trade that is far less damaging than allowing the opponent to play there.\n\nReason: To prevent the opponent from playing E3 -> D3, which would flip three of my pieces (C2, D2, E2). Moving to D3 blocks this threat and captures E3. While the opponent may recapture, it prevents a larger loss.\n\nAnswer: (D,2) -> (D,3)"}
